# skills/test-strength/targets/prove-engine.target
# The engine measures itself: mutate prove.sh, and require its own self-test to
# notice. This is the build spec's section C, mechanised.
#
# THE BOOTSTRAP LIMIT, STATED RATHER THAN PAPERED OVER. prove.sh cannot catch a
# defect that blinds it the same way twice — a mutation that removes a check AND
# removes the assertion about that check is invisible from in here. The
# independent judge is tests/skill/prove/prove.test.sh, whose expected exit codes are
# hand-written and do not use this engine's machinery to decide pass or fail.
# That asymmetry is the design, not a gap this target can close.
#
# THE LIMIT WAS NARROWED ON 2026-08-30, NOT CLOSED, AND THE DIFFERENCE MATTERS.
# `--self-test` block C now asserts that the verdict words of three fixtures are
# three DIFFERENT words rather than comparing each to a literal, so a global
# rename — `s/SURVIVORS/PROVEN/g`, which used to leave the self-test at 20
# passed 0 failed while a real run printed PROVEN above 33 listed survivors — now
# turns it red with no literal for the sed to move. What that still cannot pin is
# WHICH word is which: any rename that keeps the three distinct passes. Only a
# file the mutation cannot reach can pin the label contract, and that file is
# tests/skill/prove/prove.test.sh. Do not read block C as closing the bootstrap limit.
#
# WHY --self-test AND NOT tests/skill/prove/prove.test.sh. The self-test is hermetic: it
# builds its fixtures in a mktemp dir and reads nothing from the repo, so a
# mutant engine cannot pass by accident because some repo file happened to be
# right. Pointing this at the suite would also make the target depend on a file
# in another owner's tree, which is the cross-repo shape contracts/gates.md
# rules out one directory over.
#
# `sites 2` IS A COST DECISION AND IT IS VISIBLE. Every capped class shows
# `capped from N sites` and a capped run can never print a bare PROVEN. Each run
# is a full self-test — seconds, not milliseconds — and there is no `timeout` on
# this box to bound a mutant that loops, so the sweep is kept small deliberately
# and the cap is reported rather than hidden.

# `cost` IS RE-MEASURED WHEN THE SELF-TEST GROWS, NOT LEFT AT ITS FIRST VALUE.
# Measured 2026-08-30 with `date +%s` either side: 8s at 20 cases, 19s at 38.
# The declaration is `10` no longer, because every run of this target is a whole
# self-test and the budget arithmetic (cost x runs) is what decides whether the
# target is run at all — a stale `cost` makes an over-budget target look
# affordable and a cheap one look prohibitive. Re-measure after adding cases.

target        prove-engine
subject-kind  posix-shell
lane          deep
cost          20
sites         2
seed          repo:skills/test-strength/bin/prove.sh
place         $WORK/prove.sh
checker-cmd   sh $WORK/prove.sh --self-test || exit 1
green         0
red           1

# `|| exit 1` IS LOAD-BEARING AND IT COSTS SOMETHING. Say so, because the cost
# is a discrimination this engine has everywhere else.
#
# The canary replaces the subject with `exit 7`, and here the subject IS the
# checker — so the canary run exits 7, which is neither green (0) nor red (1),
# and the target reported COULD-NOT-LOOK with a surviving canary. That was the
# canary doing exactly its job: it said the checker does not answer in the
# vocabulary the declaration claims. Measured 2026-08-30, before this line
# existed.
#
# The fix is to say what is actually true of a self-test: ANY non-zero is a
# refusal. `0 = every case passed` is its whole contract, so a mutant that
# crashes has failed the same way a mutant that reports a failure has. What that
# gives up is the engine's usual "a crash is not a kill" rule — for this one
# target, exit 9 and exit 1 are the same verdict. That rule exists to stop a
# broken checker manufacturing recall, and it is safe to drop here only because
# the checker and the subject are the same file: a crash in it IS the defect
# being measured, not noise around the measurement.

mutate  CMP     comparison-flip
mutate  WIDE    threshold-widen
mutate  NARROW  threshold-narrow
mutate  GUARD   guard-clause-delete
mutate  EXIT    exit-neuter
mutate  NEG     negation-drop
mutate  MSG     message-drop

plant   CTL-1   sed -e 's/^  \[ "$(_lines "$P\/controls")" -ge 1 \]/  [ 1 -ge 1 ]/' "$SEED" > "$MUTANT"
        why:    delete the zero-control refusal and B1 must stop being exit 5 — the layer that makes a control mandatory before a byte is mutated
plant   INERT-1 sed -e 's/if cmp -s "$SEED" "$_mf"; then/if false; then/' -e 's/if cmp -s "$SEED" "$MUTANT"; then/if false; then/' "$SEED" > "$MUTANT"
        why:    EVERY SHAPE OF THE RULE, not one — the first version neutralised only the generated-mutant arm, B5 exercises the PLANT arm, and the mutant survived while the check it names was still running. That is the floor-equiv finding one file over: a rule with two implementations needs a mutation that reaches both, or the suite reads as unable to fail when in fact the mutation was incomplete

# RECLASSIFIED FROM A PLANT ON 2026-08-30, AFTER IT SURVIVED AND THE SURVIVAL
# WAS REPRODUCED BY HAND. Deleting the run-counter check changes no observable
# behaviour of the pristine engine, because the identity it guards
# (runs == 1 baseline + 1 canary + controls + mutants) holds by construction —
# the counter is a tripwire for a state a correct engine never reaches. So no
# SINGLE mutation of this file can make its absence visible; it takes a second
# one that actually skips a phase, built from outside — which is what
# tests/skill/prove/prove.test.sh does, by dropping a `RUNS=$((RUNS + 1))` and requiring the
# report to say `run counter mismatch`. NO ARM ID IS NAMED HERE ON PURPOSE: an
# id written into this file is a copy of a fact that lives in that one, and a
# copy drifts silently while reading correctly. Grep the suite for the reason
# string instead. Left as a plant this would be a permanent survivor, and a
# permanent survivor teaches a reader to skim the survivor list, which is the
# one thing this report cannot afford.
manual  RUNS-1   the run-counter tripwire guards a state a correct engine never reaches, so its absence is invisible to any single mutation of this file; only a fixture built OUTSIDE the engine, which skips a phase deliberately and requires the report to say "run counter mismatch", can measure it
manual  DOUBLE-1 a defect that blinds the engine and its own self-test in the same way is invisible from here by construction; tests/skill/prove/prove.test.sh is the independent judge and its expected exits are hand-written
manual  TIME-1   a mutation that makes the engine HANG is bounded but never scored: PROVE_RUN_TIMEOUT kills it and the target reports COULD-NOT-LOOK, because nobody watched the mutant finish and a guess in the generous direction is how recall gets manufactured

# A cmd control, because a valid-but-unusual INPUT to a shell subject is an
# unusual INVOCATION of it. Both of these are lawful, both must stay green, and
# both are the shapes a careless tightening breaks first.
control MUTATORS cmd sh $SUBJECT --mutators
        varies: shape
        why:    --mutators applies nothing and runs no checker, so it must work with no target directory, no workspace and no repo around it — it is what lint C6 reads
control HELP     cmd sh $SUBJECT --help
        varies: shape
        why:    a hand-invocable script that cannot answer --help is one nobody can use without reading it (lint S4), and --help must not need a valid declaration to exist
